tel 1.0

base larceny
import soundness

info   a compiler plugin which captures the compile errors and warnings of a block as values, for testing
detail
    _Larceny_ makes the failure to compile testable. Code wrapped in `demilitarize` is compiled
    separately, and the errors and warnings that fall inside it are delivered to the program as
    a `List[CompileError]` instead of failing the build. A test can then assert that an invalid
    literal, a wrong-currency addition or an undeclared relation genuinely does not compile:
    where invariants live in types, the impossibility is the feature, and this is how it is
    tested.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    test(m"a type error is captured as an error"):
      demilitarize:
        val int: Int = "not an int"
      . map(_.reason)
    . assert(_ == List(CompileError.Reason.TypeMismatch))
    ```

    The library is a compiler plugin, and `demilitarize` does nothing without it: the plugin
    must be enabled for every compilation whose sources use it, by passing its jar to the
    compiler as `-Xplugin:<path to the larceny plugin jar>` (the jar's `plugin.properties`
    names `larceny.LarcenyPlugin`). The plugin is published as a standalone artifact rather
    than in a Soundness bundle, because a `-Xplugin` jar must declare exactly one plugin; it
    must also be on the compile classpath, since `demilitarize` and `CompileError` are ordinary
    symbols. Soundness's own test modules add both.

    The plugin runs after parsing and before typing. For each compilation unit containing a
    `demilitarize` block, it compiles the same source again in a sub-compilation, on the same
    classpath and with the same language settings, prelude (`-Yimports`, `-Yno-predef`),
    `-Ycc-new`, `-Z` features, `-preview`, warning flags (`-deprecation`, `-feature`,
    `-unchecked`, `-Wall`, `-Wunused`, `-Wconf`) and other plugins, but without itself. The
    diagnostics whose point falls inside each block are captured and replaced, in the main
    compilation, by code constructing the corresponding `CompileError` values; typing then sees
    only those constructions, so the build succeeds as long as nothing outside a
    `demilitarize` block is wrong. `-Werror` is deliberately not propagated, so warnings are
    captured as warnings.

    An error in an early phase stops later phases running. So that one failing block cannot
    hide the diagnostics of another, the plugin replaces each block containing an error with
    `{}` and recompiles, as many times as needed, which is why a file with many
    `demilitarize` blocks compiles more slowly than its size suggests. Within one block, an
    error suppresses that block's late-phase warnings (deprecation among them), which are
    never reached.

    Inside a `demilitarize` block, calls through inline extension sugar may dealias opaque
    collections in proxy types and report a spurious mismatch between `scala.collection` and
    `proscenium` types; call the underlying method directly in such a block.

    The library is the single JVM-only source set `larceny.plugin`, which depends on the Scala
    compiler and nothing of Soundness's. `CompileError` and `demilitarize` are exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella.

refer larceny.demilitarize
refer larceny.CompileError
refer larceny.LarcenyPlugin

entry .demilitarize   compiles a block separately and returns its compile errors and warnings as values
    `demilitarize(code)` is rewritten by the larceny plugin, before typing, into the
    `List[CompileError]` of every diagnostic whose point lies inside `code`, in the order the
    compiler reported them. Code which compiles cleanly gives an empty list. The block itself
    is never typed in the main compilation, so it may contain anything that parses.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    @deprecated("superseded by `current`", "0.1.0")
    def obsolete(): Int = 42

    test(m"warnings can be filtered out, leaving the errors"):
      demilitarize:
        obsolete()
        val int: Int = "not an int"
      . filter(_.error).map(_.reason)
    . assert(_ == List(CompileError.Reason.TypeMismatch))
    ```

    Without the plugin enabled the call is an ordinary method, and returns an empty list:
    every assertion of a failure would then fail, rather than silently pass. A block with an
    error reports none of its own late-phase warnings, since compilation of the block stops
    at the error.

entry .procrastinate   defers compiling a block of source to run time, through the `Subcompiler`
    The plugin rewrites `procrastinate(code)` into a call on `Subcompiler` carrying the
    block's source text and the classpath, so that the code is compiled when the program runs
    rather than when it is built. Without the plugin it returns its argument.

entry .CompileError   one diagnostic captured by `demilitarize`: its reason, message, position and severity
  detail
      A case class describing a single compiler error, warning or informational message from
      within a `demilitarize` block. `#reason` is the compiler's own message identifier, as a
      `CompileError.Reason`, which is the most robust thing to assert on; `#message` is its
      full text; `#focus` is the source text the diagnostic spans; and `#importance` says
      whether it is an error, a warning or information.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      @deprecated("superseded by `current`", "0.1.0")
      def obsolete(): Int = 42

      test(m"a deprecation warning names the deprecated method"):
        demilitarize:
          obsolete()
        . map(_.message)
      . assert(_.exists(_.contains("obsolete")))
      ```

      Positions are taken from the user's source even for a diagnostic raised inside an
      inline or macro expansion: the plugin takes the narrowest position in the compilation
      unit which lies within the call site, so `#focus` is, for example, the bad splice in an
      interpolator rather than the whole literal.
  refer larceny.demilitarize
  refer larceny.CompileError.Reason
  entry .apply   a `CompileError` from a reason ordinal, message, focus, start, offset and severity level
      The form the plugin generates. An ordinal newer than `CompileError.Reason` knows becomes
      `NoExplanation`, and a level outside the known range an `Error`, so that a diagnostic
      from a newer compiler is still captured rather than silently dropped.
  entry .Importance   the severity of a captured diagnostic: `Info`, `Warning` or `Error`
    detail
        An enum whose cases mirror the Scala compiler's diagnostic levels and are named as
        `anthology.Importance`'s are. It is restated here because the plugin depends on
        nothing of Soundness's.
    entry .apply   the importance for a compiler diagnostic level, treating an unknown level as `Error`
  entry .Reason   the compiler's identifier for the kind of a diagnostic, such as `TypeMismatch`
    detail
        An enum with one case for each of the Scala 3 compiler's `ErrorMessageID`s, in the
        same order, so that a captured diagnostic can be matched by kind rather than by the
        wording of its message: `TypeMismatch`, `NotAMember`, `MissingImplicitArgument`,
        `AmbiguousOverload`, `PatternMatchExhaustivity`, `MatchCaseUnreachable`, and so on.
        `NoExplanation` is the reason of a diagnostic with no specific identifier, which
        includes every error a macro raises with `halt` and every `compiletime.error`. The
        cases marked _old_ in the source are retired identifiers kept for their position.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        test(m"a missing member is reported by its reason"):
          demilitarize:
            t"text".noSuchMember
          . map(_.reason)
        . assert(_ == List(CompileError.Reason.NotAMember))
        ```
  entry .Id   an extractor matching a `CompileError` by its `CompileError.Reason`
      `case CompileError.Id(reason) =>` binds the error's reason; it always matches. It was
      formerly the separate type `CompileErrorId`.
  entry #reason   the compiler's identifier for the kind of diagnostic, as a `CompileError.Reason`
  entry #message   the full text of the diagnostic, as the compiler wrote it
  entry #focus   the source text spanned by the diagnostic's position
  entry #start   the offset in the source file at which the diagnostic's span starts
  entry #offset   the distance of the diagnostic's point from the start of its span
  entry #importance   whether the diagnostic is an error, a warning or information
  entry #point   the offset in the source file of the diagnostic's point: `#start` plus `#offset`
  entry #warning   whether the diagnostic is a warning
  entry #error   whether the diagnostic is an error

entry .LarcenyPlugin   the compiler plugin class, named in larceny's `plugin.properties`
  detail
      The `StandardPlugin` the compiler instantiates when larceny's jar is passed with
      `-Xplugin:`. It installs a single phase, `LarcenyTransformer`, and is not used directly.
  refer larceny.LarcenyTransformer
  entry #name   the plugin's name, `larceny`

entry .LarcenyTransformer   the plugin phase which finds `demilitarize` blocks and substitutes their errors
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. A phase named `errorcap`, running after `parser` and before
      `typer`, which collects the regions of every `demilitarize` block in a unit, runs the
      `Subcompiler` over the unit's source, and rewrites each block into the construction of
      its captured `CompileError`s.
  entry .isLarceny   whether a `-Xplugin` path (jar or directory) is the larceny plugin itself
      Read from the path's `plugin.properties`; it is how the plugin keeps itself out of its
      own sub-compilations, which would otherwise recurse.
  entry #phaseName   the phase's name, `errorcap`

entry .Subcompiler   the sub-compilation that compiles a source and collects its diagnostics
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `.compile` compiles a source with a fresh compiler, collecting
      every diagnostic as a `CompileError`; given regions, it blanks each one containing an
      error and recompiles until no new errors appear, so that one block's error cannot hide
      another's diagnostics.
  entry .compile   compiles a source and returns its diagnostics as `CompileError`s
  entry .CustomReporter   the compiler reporter which records diagnostics as `CompileError`s
    detail
        Places each diagnostic at the narrowest position in the compilation unit's own source
        which lies within the user's call site.
    entry #errors   the diagnostics recorded so far
    entry #doReport   records one diagnostic as a `CompileError`
